<h2>Twixt Commentator FAQ (Fake Asked Questions)</h2>
<p><b>About Twixt Commentator</b></p>
<p>I've learned a lot about Twixt by following other players' games on Little Golem. However, it was always like watching a sports game with the sound turned off: no commentary, no analysis, no explanations. You just had to guess.</p>
<p>Twixt Commentator aims to fix that. Now commentary can be attached to a game, thereby making it easier for people to learn and improve their Twixt skills.</p>
<p>Some people like to use Twixt Commentator as a convenient way to analyze their in-progress games. This was not the original intent of the site, but I can't stop you. You can't add comments to a game until it is over, however.</p>
<p>Twixt Commentator sticks to familiar Little Golem styles. It is like a mix of two Little Golem pages:</p>
<ul>
  <li>It is like a Little Golem forum, except that every topic is a game, and the comments are all accompanied by a Twixt board so that you can follow along. Game moves in the comments are automatically converted into clickable links to the game.</li>
  <li>It is also like viewing a Little Golem game, except that you can add comments, and you can freely explore variations that did not happen in the game.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>What do R, F, L, and D mean in the End column?</b></p>
<p>These are shorthand codes for how the game ended. Most games end in resignation (R). A game can also end by Forfeit (somebody ran out of time), Loss (the opponent actually connected his sides of the board), or Draw (which must be agreed upon).</p>
<p><b>How do you cancel a comment?</b></p>
<p>When the comment box is empty, the "Comment" button turns into a "No Comment" button. A quick keyboard shortcut to empty the comment box is Ctrl+A, Backspace.</p>
<p><b>How do I make automatically clickable moves in the comments?</b></p>
<p>Anything that looks like a Twixt move (such as 23.L4, 1.i6, 2.swap, 37.resign, 27.???, etc. (the move number is required)) is automatically highlighted and becomes clickable. You can type them in just like that; they will be recognized. The most reliable way is to cut-and-paste your variation from the text that appears when you can recreate your variation on the board.</p>
<p>Watch out&mdash;moves in the comments can be ambiguous. Make it clear what variation you are talking about. Twixt Commentator makes its best guess, which means:</p>
<ol>
  <li>If the numbering sequence fits with previous numbering in the comments, it assumes you mean a continuation of that. For example, "23.g7 24.g8" will result in a link on "23.g7", and a separate link on "24.g8" that tries to guarantee "23.g7" first. If you write "23.g7, and then 24.g8 or 24.g6", then both "24.g8" and "24.g6" will try to place the "23.g7" peg first. I have found this to be the best compromise. This logic does not cross comment boundaries.</li>
  <li>Otherwise, it assumes that you mean a branch off of the main variation.</li>
</ol>
<p>Because of this ambiguity, whenever you really do mean a main branch off of the main variation, you should put a vertical bar (|) in front of your moves. For example,</p>
<p>|23.g7</p>
<p>means that 23.g7 follows whatever move 22 was in the actual game. There is no ambiguity here. Also, you can avoid ambiguity by including your entire variation in your comment.</p>
<p>For more complex discussions, and especially for blank boards, it may be useful to name a variation. This is done simply by putting the name in brackets directly after the variation. For example,</p>
<p>23.g7 [v-1]</p>
<p>Spaces before the bracket are optional. Now you can branch off of that variation anytime after its definition, even in following comments, by starting your sequence with the variation name in vertical bars, like this:</p>
<p>|v-1| 24.h9</p>
<p>Variation names must start with a letter, contain only letters, numbers, and dashes, and are case-sensitive.</p>
<p>I recommend using "Show Preview" and testing all of your moves.</p>
<p>In Size 24 games, moves are case-insensitive, so 20.D12 is the same as 20.d12. But on larger boards (sizes 30 and 48), moves are NOT case-insensitive, since the notation on the right side of the board is uppercase.</p>
<p>Blank boards support link removal, and with that comes link removal notation, which can seem cryptic at first, so here is an explanation.
  The notation uses the coordinates of link centers. One of those coordinates will always fall at a halfway point on the grid, so to avoid
  typing ".5" or "½", an apostrophe is used as shorthand — for example, e'8 or e8' refer to a link center location. Link removal is denoted
  by "-", but link addition must also specify direction, so "/" and "" are used instead. For example, -e8' refers to removing the link
  between d9 and f8, or between d8 and f9 — unambiguous because links cannot cross when link removal is on. For link addition, /e8' adds
  the link between d9 and f8, while \e8' adds the link between d8 and f9. The link notation sequence follows order of operations, always
  beginning with link removal and ending with peg placement. While links can technically be added after placing a peg, Commentator
  optimizes the sequence so that peg placement always comes last, and expects that ordering in the comments.
</p>
<p>Also, you can use some safe HTML tags, such as &lt;i&gt; and &lt;b&gt;, but not others, such as &lt;script&gt;.</p>
<p><b>Private Notes</b></p>
<p>Private notes are very similar to comments, except that nobody else can see them,
deletions are permanent, and private notes can only be added to in-progress games.
Of course, notes can coexist with comments if the game ends after adding notes,
and someone adds a comment. But named variations do not carry over, for one obvious reason:
nobody else sees your private notes.
<p>
Private notes are stored in browser local storage on your machine. They do not go to Commentator
at all. So please be aware of your browser's local storage policies. In Safari, for example,
your notes will disappear in 7 days, whereas on Chrome they are permanent until you explicitly
delete them. If you ever clear your browsing data, you'll have to be careful about whether you
are deleting your notes.
Another limitation is that your notes are only stored in one browser, on one machine.
Your notes do not transfer.
</p>
<p><b>Time Zones</b></p>
<p>The time zone is UTC, even though the server is in the Eastern US Time Zone.</p>
<p><b>What if I forget my password?</b></p>
<p>Since I don't keep anyone's e-mail address, and the password is stored with one-way encryption, there is really no way for me to recover your password. The only secure thing is to... remember your password!</p>
<p><b>About JTwixt</b></p>
<p>There is a "Download JTwixt file" link under every game board. JTwixt is a separate Java program that you can use to analyze board positions. It is much more powerful for analyzing Twixt game trees and keeping notes about branches.</p>
